We know that the artificial frequency of a sky wave for reflection from an ionospheric layer of the atmosphere is given by, $v_c=9 N^{1 / 2}$ where $n$ is the number density of electrons $/ \mathrm{m}^3$. It is given that $N=10^{11} / \mathrm{m}^3$
\therefore \quad v_c=9 \times\left(10^{11}\right)^{1 / 2}=2.8 \times 10^6 \mathrm{~Hz}=2.8 \mathrm{MHz} \text {. }

Hence the wave of frequency $2 \mathrm{MHz}$ will be reflected back.

According to Gauss's law, the total electric flux through a closed surface is given by

\phi=\oint_s \vec{E} \cdot d \vec{s}=\frac{Q}{\varepsilon_0}

where $Q$ is the total charge enclosed by the surface. The total charge enclosed by the given surface is $Q=+q-q=0 . \quad \therefore$ The total flux $\phi=0$.

For a projectile to leave the gravitational field of the earth, its kinetic energy would be at least equal to its potential energy. That is

\frac{1}{2} m v_e^2=G \frac{M m}{R} \Rightarrow v_e=\sqrt{\frac{2 G M}{R}}

where $G$ is the universal gravitational constant, $M$ is the mass of the earth and $R$ is the radius of the earth. Hence the escape velocity of a projectile is independent of its own mass.

The magnetic field at the centre of a circular loop carrying current $I$ is given by $B=\frac{m_0 I}{2 r}$ where $r$ is the radius of the circular loop. Given that $r=5.0 \mathrm{~cm}=0.05 \mathrm{~m}$.
Now, $B=\frac{\mu_0 I}{2 r}=0.5 \times 10^{-5}$
\begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow \quad I & =\frac{0.5 \times 10^{-5} \times 2 \times 0.05}{\mu_0}=\frac{0.5 \times 10^{-5} \times 0.1}{4 \pi \times 10^{-7}} \\
& =0.3978 \approx 0.4 Å
\end{aligned}

